</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/5213</identifier><datestamp>2026-09-28T13:25:58Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_223</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>As decisões estruturais na jurisdição constitucional brasileira: a utilização das medidas estruturais como técnicas de correção de um “estado de coisas em desconformidade com a Constituição”</dc:title>
   <dc:title>Structural Reform in Brazilian Constitutional Adjudication: the use of structural remedies as techniques for overcoming a “Unconstitutional State of Affairs”</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Marina de Souza Pompermayer</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Direito constitucional</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Controle da constitucionalidade</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Direitos fundamentais</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Brasil. Supremo Tribunal Federal</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Separação de poderes</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Jurisdição constitucional</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Decisões estruturais</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Processo estrutural</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Controle concentrado de constitucionalidade;</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Estado de coisas inconstitucional</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Direitos fundamentais</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Supremo Tribunal Federal</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Separação de poderes</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>This study aims to reconstruct the reception by the Brazilian Supreme Federal Court (Supremo Tribunal Federal) of structural litigation techniques within constitutional review, from the perspective of correcting a “state of affairs incompatible with the Constitution.” It begins from the diagnosis that the Court has increasingly been called upon to address systematic and recurrent violations of fundamental rights whose persistence stems from the very functioning of the state’s bureaucratic apparatus. This context has led the Court to adopt judicial techniques aimed at restructuring public policies and institutions. The dissertation critically examines this practice along three central axes: the conception of litigation and procedure underlying structural decisions; the understanding of the fundamental rights that may be subject to structural judicial protection; and the criteria of legitimacy of constitutional adjudication within the proceduralist paradigm of law. To this end, the study reconstructs the concept of structural litigation in light of both domestic and foreign scholarship, with particular attention to the North American and Colombian experiences, as well as to critiques formulated from the perspective of polycentric conflicts. At the theoretical level, the dissertation draws on the proceduralist paradigm of law, particularly as developed in Jürgen Habermas’s discursive theory, in order to assess the normative limits of judicial action in contexts of structural violations of rights. In addition, it undertakes an analysis of ADPFs 347, 635, and 991 to highlight the foundations of the Supreme Federal Court’s decision-making practice, pointing to the need for rigorous criteria for structural remedies. The study concludes that structural decisions are constitutionally appropriate only when they are articulated with a procedural conception of adjudication, a precise understanding of the rights at stake, and a democratic model of constitutional adjudication.</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2026-09-28T13:25:58Z</dc:date>
   <dc:date>2026-02-05</dc:date>
   <dc:type>Dissertação de mestrado</dc:type>
