</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/3944</identifier><datestamp>2026-08-05T03:00:13Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_406</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Estudo Transversal de Manifestações Psiquiátricas em Pacientes Brasileiros com Doença de Huntington</dc:title>
   <dc:title>Cross-Sectional Study of Psychiatric Manifestations in Brazilian Patients with Huntington’s Disease</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Débora Cardoso Corrêa</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Neurociências</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Doença de huntington</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Estudos Transversais</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Comportamento</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Transtornos Mentais</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Doença de Huntington</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Estudo transversal</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Alterações comportamentais</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Manifestações psiquiátricas</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>Huntington’s disease (HD) is a hereditary neurodegenerative disorder with autosomal dominant inheritance, characterized by a broad clinical spectrum that includes motor, cognitive, and psychiatric manifestations. Although motor symptoms are classically regarded as the diagnostic hallmark, growing evidence indicates that psychiatric and behavioral disturbances may precede or accompany the disease throughout its course, exerting a significant impact on global functioning and quality of life. Despite their clinical relevance, these manifestations remain insufficiently explored in the Brazilian population. The aim of this study was to determine the prevalence and characterize the clinical profile of psychiatric and behavioral manifestations in Brazilian patients with HD, as well as to analyze their association with motor, cognitive, and functional impairment and health-related quality of life. This cross-sectional observational study included 55 patients with genetically confirmed HD followed at a specialized center. Participants were assessed using the Unified Huntington’s Disease Rating Scale (UHDRS), Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA), Frontal Assessment Battery (FAB), Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), Hamilton Depression and Anxiety Scales (HAM-D and HAM-A), Young Mania Rating Scale (YMRS), Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS), Mini International Neuropsychiatric Interview (MINI), Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (CSSRS), Short Form-36 (SF-36), and the Fagerström Test for Nicotine Dependence. A high prevalence of psychiatric manifestations was observed across different clinical stages of HD. Generalized anxiety disorder was present in 60.0% of participants, major depressive disorder in 50.9%, and depressive symptoms in up to 58.2% according to the HAM-D. Psychotic symptoms were identified in 23.6% of the sample, irritability in 25.5%, perseverative/obsessive behaviors in 23.6%, and suicidal ideation in 12.7%. Psychiatric manifestations were also observed in early functional stages (I/II), which accounted for 67.2% of the sample. Depression, assessed both dimensionally and categorically, was associated with poorer functional performance, greater motor severity, and reduced functional independence (p &lt; 0.01). Psychosis showed a robust association with greater clinical severity, including worse motor and functional scores, lower independence, longer disease duration, and poorer global and executive cognitive performance (MoCA and FAB; p &lt; 0.05). In multivariate analysis, psychiatric diagnosis, depressive symptoms (HAM-D), and anxiety symptoms (HAM-A) remained independently associated with poorer quality of life, explaining 37.1% of the variance in the global SF-36 score. These findings demonstrate that psychiatric and behavioral manifestations constitute a central and intrinsic component of the clinical phenotype of Huntington’s disease, with a significant impact on functionality, cognition, and quality of life, regardless of motor severity. The results underscore the need for integrated clinical approaches and systematic psychiatric assessment throughout the entire course of the disease, contributing to more comprehensive and patient-centered care strategies.</dc:description>
