</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/3259</identifier><datestamp>2026-07-13T15:06:25Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_207</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Avaliação do potencial psicobiótico de Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus 64 em modelo murino de compulsão alimentar</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Gabriella Fabiane de Assis Malta</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Bioquímica e imunologia</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Transtorno da Compulsão Alimentar</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Sistema Mesolímbico</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Probióticos</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Eixo Intestino-Cérebro</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Transtorno de compulsão alimentar</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Sistema mesolímbico</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus 64</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Probiótico</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Eixo microbiota-intestino-cérebro</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>Binge eating disorder (BED) is the most prevalent eating disorder in Western populations,
affecting approximately 0.6% to 6.1% of women and 0.3% to 0.7% of men. It is characterized
by recurrent episodes of excessive food intake, particularly of highly palatable foods,
accompanied by loss of control and emotional distress. These foods, rich in sugars and fats,
activate the mesolimbic and mesocortical dopaminergic systems, which are associated with the
sensation of reward. In addition to an increased risk for metabolic disorders such as obesity and
diabetes mellitus, BED is frequently comorbid with psychiatric conditions, including anxiety
and depression. Currently, lisdexamfetamine (LDX) is the only drug approved for the treatment
of BED, although other medications, such as fluoxetine, are also used in clinical practice.
However, as a psychostimulant, LDX carries a potential risk of abuse, highlighting the need for
novel therapeutic alternatives.
In this context, manipulation of the gut–brain axis has emerged as a potential intervention, given
evidence that intestinal microorganisms regulate appetite, body mass, and mood. Thus, it was
hypothesized that treatment with the potential probiotic Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus 64 (L64)
would be able to reduce the consumption of a hyperpalatable diet in a murine model of binge
eating. To this end, male and female C57BL/6 mice were subjected to twelve binge eating
sessions, three times per week, for two hours, receiving either a control diet (AIN-93G) or a
highly palatable diet (HSB). Some animals underwent only the binge eating protocol, whereas
others received vehicle, L64, or fluoxetine via gavage for seven days, either before or after the
binge sessions. Behavioral tests assessing anxiety-, compulsivity-, and depression-like
behaviors were also conducted, along with measurements of body weight, adiposity index, and
fasting blood glucose.
Both sexes developed binge eating; however, only females exhibited behavioral alterations
associated with anxiety and compulsivity, as well as glycemic changes. Administration of L64
reduced the intake of the highly palatable diet exclusively in females treated after the binge
eating protocol, although the gavage procedure may have influenced the observed behavior. In
males, no behavioral changes were detected, but post-binge treatment with L64 reduced
glutamatergic neurotransmission, which had been previously elevated by HSB diet
consumption. Overall, the results demonstrate sex-specific differences in response to both the
binge eating model and L64 treatment, suggesting that factors such as sex, timing of
intervention, route of administration, and treatment duration influence therapeutic efficacy.
Future studies should explore longer treatment periods and strategies that minimize stress
associated with gavage administration.</dc:description>
   <dc:description>CAPES - Coordenação de Aperfeiçoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior</dc:description>
