</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/2893</identifier><datestamp>2026-05-26T16:18:46Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_110</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Avaliação da atividade anti-hemostática e anti-complemento da saliva de Lutzomyia longipalpis na presença de soro de hospedeiros previamente imunizados pela picada do inseto</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Isabella Góes Mantini da Cunha</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Parasitologia</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Psychodidae</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Anticorpos</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Proteínas e Peptídeos Salivares</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Flebotomíneos</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Anticorpos</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Lutzomyia longipalpis</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Proteínas salivares</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>Phlebotomines are dipterans, belonging to the Psychodidae family and the Phlebotominae sub-family, whose females perform a blood meal for maturation of the
ovaries. During the blood meal, these insects can be vectors of microorganisms, including the protozoa of the genus Leishmania sp. The habits of insects, as well as
their vertebrate hosts, vary by species. Insects of the species Lutzomyia longipalpis feed on various hosts such as chickens, foxes, domestic dogs, rodents and humans.
This species is the main vector of Leishmania infantum in the New World, the etiologic agent of American Visceral Leishmaniasis (AVL). For this reason, it is an
insect of great medical and veterinary interest. The hematophagy process can be challenging for the insect, so there are a number of factors in its physiology that aid
in the ingestion and digestion of blood. One of the devices used by hematophagous arthropods to facilitate this process is their saliva. The saliva is constituted of proteins
that have anti-hemostatic and immunomodulatory properties and, in general, vertebrate hosts that come in contact with this saliva produce anti-saliva antibodies.
The main objective in this work was to investigate the functionality of female Lutzomyia longipalpis saliva in the presence of these antibodies, in other words,
evaluate if the antibodies could bind to the saliva proteins, inhibiting its anti-hemostatic and immunomodulatory properties. It was observed that, given the
method of immunization chosen (insect bites), the mouse serum produced antibodies and these antibodies were reactive to 3 salivary protein bands. However, none of the
salivary functions evaluated were affected. This means that some of the saliva proteins remain functional when Lutzomyia longipalpis feeds on animals previously bitten and immunized, allowing hematophagy to occur. This work represents a portion of the data being produced by our group, and is of great importance because it helps to elucidate physiological factors of the insect. The goal is, at the end of the studies, to propose strategies for the production of vaccines that impair blood intake and reduce the life span of infected insects.</dc:description>
   <dc:description>CNPq - Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2026-05-26T16:18:45Z</dc:date>
