</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/2910</identifier><datestamp>2026-05-27T14:35:30Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_112</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Desvendando a dinâmica neurovascular na disfunção colinérgica: uma investigação por ultrassom funcional em camundongos VAChT knockdown</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Ana Caroline Nogueira Souza</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Doença de Alzheimer</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Neuroimagem Funcional</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Ultrassonografia Doppler</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Acetilcolina</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Proteínas Vesiculares de Transporte de Acetilcolina</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Dissertação Acadêmica</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Sistema colinérgico</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Doença de Alzheimer</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Acoplamento neurovascular</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Ultrassom funcional</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Conectividade funcional</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>Cholinergic neurotransmission plays a central role in modulating cortical activity,
functional connectivity (FC), and neurovascular coupling (NVC), and is widely implicated
in the pathophysiology of Alzheimer’s disease (AD). The vesicular acetylcholine
transporter (VAChT) is essential for packaging acetylcholine into synaptic vesicles, and
its reduction leads to decreased cholinergic release. In this study, we investigated the
impact of VAChT reduction on cerebral hemodynamics and functional brain organization
using functional ultrasound (fUS), a high spatiotemporal resolution technique capable of
measuring cerebral blood volume at rest and during sensory stimulation. In the first
experiment, a longitudinal study was conducted with VAChT KDHET, VAChT KDHOM, and
VAChT WT mice between 6 and 12 weeks of age, a period preceding the onset of
detectable cognitive deficits. Resting-state FC analysis revealed preservation of the global
connectivity pattern among the hippocampus, cortex, and basal forebrain, with maintained
interhemispheric connectivity and strong cortico-visual coupling. Visual stimulation elicited
consistent activation of the classical visual pathway (visual cortex, lateral geniculate
nucleus, and superior colliculus), with no statistically significant differences between
genotypes after correction for multiple comparisons. In the second experiment, performed
in adult animals aged 16 to 31 weeks (mean = 23.1), the impact of the anesthetic protocol
(isoflurane or ketamine/xylazine) was assessed. The anesthetic influenced the visual
evoked response, with significant differences restricted to small cortical areas,
predominantly within visual regions. No extensive global alterations in FC were observed
between genotypes. Overall, the results indicate that VAChT reduction, at the ages
evaluated, was not associated with extensive alterations in FC or NVC detectable by fUS,
suggesting early functional preservation or the presence of compensatory mechanisms.
This study highlights the translational potential of fUS for investigating early neurovascular
alterations associated with cholinergic dysfunction and AD.</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2026-05-27T14:35:30Z</dc:date>
