</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/4508</identifier><datestamp>2026-08-29T03:00:11Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_224</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Imagens de controle e atos de resistência de atletas negras medalhistas olímpicas no Brasil : fragmentos das trajetórias de Rebeca Andrade, Rafaela Silva, Fabiana Claudino e Formiga</dc:title>
   <dc:title>Controlling images and acts of resistance by black olympic medalist athletes in Brazil : fragments of the trajectories of Rebeca Andrade, Rafaela Silva, Fabiana Claudino, and Formiga.</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Olívia Luiza Pilar de Souza</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Comunicação Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Mulheres atletas  Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Atletas negros Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Esportes Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Atletas negras</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Esporte</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Imagens de controle</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Interseccionalidade</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Resistência</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>The objective of this thesis is to investigate which controlling images affect women in Brazilian sports and to analyze the discourses of resistance to these images articulated by black female Olympic medalists. The relevance of this research emerges from the identification of a gap in studies  on  Communication  and  Sport  in  Brazil:  the  absence  of  theoretical  approaches  that privilege black intellectuals and an intersectional perspective. For the theoretical foundation, we  draw  on  the  contributions  of  Patricia  Hill  Collins  (2019a,  2004)  and  previous  research (Souza, 2021) on controlling images; Kimberlé Crenshaw (1989, 1991, 2002, 2016) and Patricia Hill Collins and Sirma Bilge (2021) on intersectionality; Lélia Gonzalez (1984, 2020) on the context  of  black  women  in  Brazil;  and  Silvana  Goellner  (1999,  2005a),  Mourão  (2000), Adelman  (2003)  and  Vimieiro  et  al.  (2021,  2023,  2025),  and  other  researchers  on  female participation  in  sports.  The  methodological  approach  is  divided  into  two  main  stages:  (1) identifying the controlling images associated with athletes in specific sports — including artistic gymnastics, judo, volleyball, and women's soccer  — represented by the media and society, through  a  literature  review  and  documentary  research;  and  (2)  analyzing  fragments  of  the narratives of the sporting trajectories of selected black female athletes  — Rebeca Andrade, Rafaela Silva, Fabiana Claudino, and Formiga (Miraildes Mota) — using documentary research in different media, to understand whether and how they resist these controlling images based on their self-explanatory statements. To this end, this thesis is organized into two theoreticalanalytical parts and nine chapters. Before the theory, we present the state of the art, the gaps, and the relevance of the research (introductory chapter), as well as the methodological design (chapter two). In the first part, we discuss the concept of controlling images in dialogue with the intersectional perspective (chapter three) and the controlling images identified in each sport (chapter  four).  In  the  second  part,  we  analyze,  in  chapters  dedicated  to  each  athlete,  the discourses  of  Rebeca  Andrade,  Rafaela  Silva,  Fabiana  Claudino,  and  Formiga  (Miraildes Mota), exploring fragments of their sporting life stories and the acts of resistance articulated in their statements. We conclude with final considerations and research findings.</dc:description>
   <dc:description>CAPES - Coordenação de Aperfeiçoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior</dc:description>
