</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/4431</identifier><datestamp>2026-08-25T18:36:34Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_192</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Estudo comparativo da titulação de isoaglutininas ABO de pools plaquetários PAS-C em tubo e esferas de vidro</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Fernanda Carolina Alves Campos Oliveira</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Patologia</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Plaquetas</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Aglutininas</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Titulometria</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Pools de plaquetas PAS-C</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Titulação de isoaglutinina IgM</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Esfera de vidro</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>BACKGROUND: ABO-incompatible platelet transfusions are the major cause of acute reactions in minor ABO incompatibility. Pathogen-inactivated PAS-C pools reduce plasma volume by 70%, but average isohemagglutinin titers and the correlation between glass bead column agglutination and tube titration remain poorly understood. STUDY DESIGN AND METHODS: 148 group O pathogen-inactivated PAS-C platelet pool samples from AABB-accredited Hemominas Foundation were refrigerated and tested within 7 days. IgM titers were measured using both manual room-temperature tube hemagglutination and automated glass bead column agglutination, and results were analyzed for concordance, correlation, and agreement (Wilcoxon and Bland–Altman) using MedCalc® v23.4.8. RESULTS: The commonest titer was 64. Anti-A titers >64 were 19,6% (tube) and 41,9% (glass bead), while anti-B were 15,5% and 31%, respectively. Concordance was high (85,1% for anti-A, 90,5% for anti-B), but agreement was low (Kappa 0.17968 and 0.12072) with weak correlations (Spearman 0.322 and 0.301). Titers differed significantly between methods (p=0.0001). Tube results tended to show higher anti-A titers than glass bead assays. Agglutination was 4+ at 1:1 in all tube tests and ranged from 3+ to 4+ (Med 3+) in glass bead assays. DISCUSSION: A relatively high proportion of titers >64 was observed. Although the assays showed good agreement, low correlations and statistical differences in pathogen-inactivated PAS-C platelet pools suggest the methods may not be interchangeable. This is the first study to measure IgM isohemagglutinin titers in these specific substrates using automated glass bead column agglutination versus manual tube hemagglutination.</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2026-08-25T18:36:34Z</dc:date>
