</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/4972</identifier><datestamp>2026-09-18T13:19:10Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_5</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_240</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Análise termoeconômica comparativa de sistemas de armazenamento de energia de longa duração entre uma usina reversível e um sistema de armazenamento de energia por ar líquido no contexto do leilão de reserva de capacidade</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Dilson Gomes Werneck</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Engenharia elétrica</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Energia - Armazenamento</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Termodinâmica</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Armazenamento de Energia Criogênico (LAES)</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Usinas reversíveis</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Engenharia econômica</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Leilão de reserva de capacidade</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Patente UFMG</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>The massive penetration of variable renewable energy sources in the Brazilian electricity mix imposes new operational paradigms on the National Interconnected System (SIN), exacerbated by the reduction in the regularization capacity of hydroelectric reservoirs. This work investigates the techno-economic feasibility of Long Duration Energy Storage (LDES) technologies for providing firm power within the scope of the Capacity Reserve Auction (LRCAP). The case study focuses on the HPP “Simulada” (located in the semi-arid region of Minas Gerais), comparing two retrofit alternatives: (i) adaptation to an Open-Loop Pumped Hydro Storage (PHS), using the existing river reservoir as the lower level and constructing an artificial upper reservoir, and (ii) implementation of a Liquid Air Energy Storage (LAES) system in an adiabatic cycle, based on UFMG Patent BR 102016005698-5. The methodology integrates detailed thermodynamic analysis and rigorous economic modeling, based on indicators such as NPV, LCOS, and Discounted Payback. The results, monetarily adjusted to 2026, indicate that although PHS presents superior round-trip efficiency (75%), the Adiabatic LAES system (55% efficiency) demonstrates greater financial robustness for the capacity market. Detailed analysis reveals that the revenue anticipation enabled by the modular construction of LAES compensates for its higher operational cost, resulting in a positive NPV and mitigating critical geological and environmental risks associated with direct intervention in the natural watercourse. The proposed national technology allows for optimized liquefaction yield, reducing CAPEX and guaranteeing availability independent of the hydrological regime, a decisive factor for energy security in the semi-arid region</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2026-09-18T13:19:10Z</dc:date>
   <dc:date>2026-02-28</dc:date>
   <dc:type>Monografia de especialização</dc:type>
