</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/4876</identifier><datestamp>2026-09-15T12:31:18Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_235</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Autoeficácia para prática de atividade física em indivíduos pós-acidente vascular cerebral: propriedades de medida de instrumento aplicado por telefone e fatores determinantes da autoeficácia</dc:title>
   <dc:title>Self-efficacy for physical activity in individuals post-stroke: measurement properties of a telephone-based instrument and factors determinants of self-efficacy</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Jéssica Melo dos Santos Ferreira</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Exercícios físicos</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Acidente vascular cerebral</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Autoeficácia</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Telemedicina</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Acidente Vascula Cerebral</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Autoeficácia</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Atividade Física</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Instrumentos de Medida</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Telessáude</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Fatores Associados</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>Self-efficacy for physical activity (PA) remains underexplored in individuals post-stroke. Investigating the measurement properties of instruments that assess this outcome remotely may enable and/or facilitate the assessment of these individuals. In addition, investigating factors associated with self-efficacy for PA post-stroke may help identify target individuals and develop strategies to improve it. Two studies were conducted. Study-1, a methodological study, investigated the agreement between face-to-face and telephone-based administrations and the inter-rater reliability of the telephone-based administration of the “Self-efficacy scale for physical activity ” in individuals post-stroke. Study-2, a cross-sectional study, investigated whether factors related to sociodemographic characteristics, health condition, and PA were associated with self-efficacy for PA in individuals post-stroke. In Study-1, agreement between face-to-face and telephone-based administrations was investigated using the intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) with 95% confidence intervals (95% CI), considering the total score and sections 1 and 2. Scatter plots were generated. Weighted Kappa (kw) assessed agreement between individual items across face-to-face and telephone-based administrations (α=5%). Inter-rater reliability of the telephone-based administrations of the instrument was investigated using the ICC with 95% CI, considering the total score and sections 1 and 2. Subsequently, the standard error of measurement (SEM) and minimal detectable change (MDC) were calculated. Bland-Altman plots were generated. The kw assessed inter-rater reliability of the telephone-based administrations for each item (α=5%). In Study-2, the dependent variable was self-efficacy for PA. Independent variables: age, occupation, living arrangement, educational level, number of medications in use, history of recurrent stroke, mean energy expenditure (EE), and exercise capacity. Three multiple linear regression models were generated for the total score and sections 1 and 2 (α=5%). Fifty-two individuals (63±11 years) were included in Study-1. Agreement between face-to-face and telephone-based administrations showed a moderate magnitude for the total score (p&lt;0.001;ICC=0.68;95%CI=0.50-0.80), section 1 (p&lt;0.001;ICC=0.61;95%CI=0.40-0.75), and section 2 (p&lt;0.001;ICC=0.50;95%CI=0.26-0.68). Scatter plots corroborated these findings. Item-level kw analysis ranged from moderate to substantial (p&lt;0.001;0.35≤kw≤0.65), except for Item 6 (p=0.129). Inter-rater reliability showed an excellent magnitude for the total score (p&lt;0.001;ICC=0.95;95%CI=0.91-0.97;SEM=0.63;MDC=1.75), section 1 (p&lt;0.001;ICC=0.95;95%CI=0.91-0.97;SEM=0.34;MDC=0.94), and section 2 (p&lt;0.001;ICC=0.91;95%CI=0.84-0.95;SEM=0.52;MDC=1.44). Bland-Altman plots corroborated these findings. Item-level kw analysis ranged from moderate to almost perfect (p&lt;0.001;0.56≤kw≤0.96). In Study-2, 91 individuals (62±13 years) were included. For the total score and section 1, higher mean EE (β=0.428;p=0.001/β=0.402;p=0.002) and higher educational level (β=0.210;p=0.046/β=0.245;p=0.024) were associated with greater self-efficacy for PA post-stroke. For section 2, only higher mean EE was associated with self-efficacy for PA (β=0.331;p=0.012). It was concluded that the instrument demonstrated measurement properties supporting its use by telephone in individuals post-stroke. The non-significant finding for item 6 may be attributed to low response variability between administrations and does not invalidate the other results. Mean EE and educational level should be considered when identifying target individuals and developing strategies aimed at increasing self-efficacy for PA post-stroke.</dc:description>
