</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/5263</identifier><datestamp>2026-09-29T15:08:26Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_292</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>"Onde a ferida mais dói" : políticas e violências urbanas a partir das experiências e das performances d'O Rappa e do Planet Hemp na década de 1990</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Rubens de Brito Ferreira Teixeira</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>História Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Música popular Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Violência urbana Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Performances Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>O Rappa (Conjunto musical) Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Planet Hemp (Conjunto musical) Teses</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Performance</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Planet Hemp</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>O Rappa</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>História e Política</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Nova República</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>This research is constituted as an examination of the social trajectories of O Rappa and Planet Hemp, exploring how they shaped and were shaped by living in Rio de Janeiro during the 1990s. The phonographic materials produced by both bands are analyzed as albums, endowed with historicity, cultural dynamics, central concepts and representations, becoming capitals of consecration within the Brazilian artistic field. Therefore, it is an investigation regarding the experiences of the members of these musical groups and how they were shaped by their socio-political context and transformed into protest songs and performances. At first, it’s made an examination of the social trajectories of O Rappa, Planet Hemp and their respective members, in order to verify the circumstances of the of the bands’ formation, the influences and references brought by each individual and how this junction provided and fed on the musical scenes and particular soundscapes of the capital of Rio de Janeiro. In the following, when defining the discs released as albums, we can observe various forms, uses and purposes of the performances, being present in the songs, inserts, interviews, testimonials and presentations, forming a particular cluster of multiple meanings. Parts of these findings revealed that, although the bands were from the same generation, were friends, and occupied the same spaces, there was no consensus among them regarding how urban violence was experienced and musicalized, just as there was no consensus regarding who might be responsible for the waves of crime and insecurity they experienced. It is argued that, by moving from the underground to the mainstream of the phonographic industry, these groups created tension in the Brazilian artistic and political fields, largely due to their debate on themes sometimes omitted even by public authorities. Ultimately, they opened paths and spaces for new meanings, representations, thinking, perceiving, and acting politically through performances that reflect the uncertainties of the New Republic.</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2026-09-29T15:08:26Z</dc:date>
   <dc:date>2026-03-30</dc:date>
