</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/3561</identifier><datestamp>2026-07-21T13:40:06Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_5</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_397</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>As artes visuais como campo de conhecimento na educação infantil</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Helena Maria da Rocha</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Arte - Estudo e ensino</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Educação de crianças</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Experiência</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Educação infantil</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Desenvolvimento</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Ensino da arte</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Artes visuais</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>This article addresses the importance of systematic teaching of Visual Arts in Early Childhood Education, raising questions about how it has been conducted at this educational stage. It discusses the contribution of art to the holistic development of children, taking as a starting point the guidelines found in the documents that guide national education. It also presents some activities proposed and carried out with a class of one to two-year-old children, in which the author is the lead teacher. The theoretical framework is based on the authors John Dewey (1979), Ana Mae Barbosa (2001), and Lucia Gouvêa Pimentel (2013), who discuss art in a broad sense. The article also draws on contributions from authors Susana Rangel Vieira da Cunha (2022), Daniela Linck Diefenthäler (2021), and Luciana Esmeralda Ostetto (2008), who research the teaching of Visual Arts specifically in Early Childhood Education. The research results indicate that the teaching of Visual Arts in Early Childhood Education has been conducted in a decontextualized manner. This highlights the need for a new approach that values children's experiences, as learning occurs precisely through these experiences.</dc:description>
