</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/3449</identifier><datestamp>2026-07-16T13:08:21Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_404</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>O lítio, seus públicos e seus problemas: dinâmicas de  alertas em torno da exploração de lítio no Vale do  Jequitinhonha</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Paula Ferreira Ribeiro</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Transição energética</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Lítio</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Jequitinhonha, Rio, Vale (MG e BA)</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Administração</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Transição energética; Cartografia de controvérsias; Descarbonização; Estudos organizacionais; Sociologia pragmática.</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>This dissertation seeks to understand how lithium extraction in the Vale do Jequitinhonha (Minas Gerais, Brazil) has been constituted as a public problem and to uncover its publics, based on the emergence of alerts and their publicization in public arenas. Grounded in a qualitative approach inspired by pragmatism, the research mobilizes the cartography of controversies and the analysis of documents, news reports, technical notes, and public hearings, allowing for the reconstruction of trajectories of problematization and reconfiguration of the controversy. The analysis highlights three major movements: the initial construction of the project as a promise of development and regional redemption; the emergence of alerts and collective experiences of being affected (such as water problems, dust, noise, illnesses, territorial transformations, and high real estate speculation); and the transition to a phase of proofs and tests, in which recommendations from the Public Prosecutor’s Office, technical notes from research groups, and institutional decisions begin to challenge the official framing. The results show that the controversy is permeated by neo-extractivism and the decarbonization consensus, in which the energy transition is used to legitimize the intensification of mining and the creation of new sacrifice zones, while communities, social movements, and researchers mobilize historical–experiential and technical-scientific regimes of proof to dispute the meaning of development and the future of the region. By articulating the sociology of public problems, the sociology of transformations, and debates on extractivism, energy transition, and socio-environmental justice, the dissertation contributes to Organizational Studies by addressing lithium mining as a sociotechnical arrangement that brings together organizations, the State, markets, and communities in the production of public problems, redefining scales of decision-making and modes of governing territories.</dc:description>
